-- Blueprint overrides for Line Wars: storage capacities, factory build and
-- upgrade costs, and the energy price of any unit whose stock energy cost is out
-- of reach here. Pure Economy MERGEs (a documented-safe target), applied by the
-- map's *.bp scan at LOAD time on both the sim and UI passes.
--
-- The energy rule of thumb: army energy storage is a flat 3900 for the whole
-- game (the ACU's, and nothing Line Wars spawns adds any) against 100 e/s of
-- income, so stock air prices — 1:20 mass:energy — make a unit unqueueable
-- rather than expensive. Everything here is normalised to the ~1:5 ratio the T1
-- land units already have; mass is left at stock so the factions keep their
-- relative pricing.
--
-- IMPORTANT (learned 2026-07-22): this MUST be done at load time via a .bp, NOT
-- at runtime in the sim. Editing __blueprints from the script (OnPopulate etc.)
-- changes only what lua reads — the engine finalises each unit's economy from the
-- C-side blueprint at load, so a runtime lua edit does NOT change a spawned unit's
-- actual storage cap (confirmed: the "granted +100 storage" log printed 100 while
-- the unit still contributed 500). Load-time .bp merges DO reach the engine.
--
-- CAVEAT: a map .bp loses to MODS, which load after the map. With unit-overhaul
-- mods active (Kamikaze's load order: blackopsfaf-unleashed, totalmayhem, ...) the ACU
-- merge below is overridden back to the mod's value and the ACU keeps ~650
-- storage. These overrides only take effect when playing WITHOUT such mods.
--
-- The OVERRIDES table below is the single source of truth for Line Wars costs and
-- is parsed by tools/gen-units-md.py to build UNITS.md — keep it in this literal
-- shape (one row per group, `ids = { ... }` plus the Economy fields to merge).

local OVERRIDES = {
    {
        -- The ACU's mass storage is the whole round-1 cap, and it is also gifted
        -- as starting mass at warp-in (ACUUnit.GiveInitialResources). Stock 650.
        ids = { 'uel0001', 'ual0001', 'url0001', 'xsl0001' },
        StorageMass = 216,
    },
    {
        -- T1 land factory. Cheapened from stock 240m/2100e so the first factory
        -- is affordable inside the round-1 cap of 216 (players build their own
        -- factories now — none is placed for them). Storage zeroed (stock 80) so
        -- owning factories doesn't quietly raise the cap.
        ids = { 'ueb0101', 'uab0101', 'urb0101', 'xsb0101' },
        BuildCostMass = 100,
        BuildCostEnergy = 500,
        StorageMass = 0,
    },
    {
        -- T1 air factory. Dearer than land: it unlocks bombers, which ignore the
        -- lane entirely. Stock 210m/2400e, storage 80.
        ids = { 'ueb0102', 'uab0102', 'urb0102', 'xsb0102' },
        BuildCostMass = 150,
        BuildCostEnergy = 800,
        StorageMass = 0,
    },
    {
        -- T1 attack bombers. Stock energy is 2050 against a 3900 energy cap and
        -- 100 e/s income, which made a second bomber unqueueable for most of a
        -- round — energy, not mass, was the whole air balance. 450 keeps the
        -- ~1:5 mass:energy ratio the T1 land units use (90 mass -> 450 energy).
        ids = { 'uea0103', 'uaa0103', 'ura0103', 'xsa0103' },
        BuildCostEnergy = 450,
    },
    {
        -- T1 interceptors (air-to-air), the counter to a bomber-heavy opponent.
        -- Stock 50m/2250e — same energy trap as the bomber against a 100 e/s
        -- income and the round cap. 250 keeps the ~1:5 mass:energy ratio the
        -- other units use (50 mass -> 250 energy), so an interceptor sits just
        -- cheaper than a bomber (90m/450e) and you can react to an air push.
        ids = { 'uea0102', 'uaa0102', 'ura0102', 'xsa0102' },
        BuildCostEnergy = 250,
    },
    {
        -- T2 land factory = the tier-2 upgrade cost. Stock 1410m/11200e is
        -- unreachable under the round cap; storage zeroed (stock 160) so
        -- upgrading doesn't quietly raise the cap.
        --
        -- !! READ THIS BEFORE TUNING ANY FACTORY ABOVE TIER 1 !!
        -- These are NOT what the player pays. Every stock factory above tier 1
        -- sets `Economy.DifferentialUpgradeCostCalculation = true`, so both the
        -- upgrade button's tooltip and FactoryQueue.UpgradeCost price the upgrade
        -- as (this tier) MINUS (the tier below). The numbers below are therefore
        -- chosen so the DIFFERENCE is the intended price:
        --   T1 land 100m/500e  -> T2 land 350m/1700e  => charged 250m/1200e
        -- (Before 2026-07-26 the script charged the full value while the button
        -- showed the difference — that is the "the upgrade cost more than the
        -- blueprint says" bug. Keep the two in step when editing.)
        ids = { 'ueb0201', 'uab0201', 'urb0201', 'xsb0201' },
        BuildCostMass = 350,
        BuildCostEnergy = 1700,
        StorageMass = 0,
    },
    {
        -- T2 air factory. Priced above the T2 land upgrade for the same reason
        -- the T1 air factory is dearer than the T1 land one: it unlocks units
        -- that ignore the lane. Storage zeroed (stock 160).
        --   T1 air 150m/800e -> T2 air 500m/2550e => charged 350m/1750e
        ids = { 'ueb0202', 'uab0202', 'urb0202', 'xsb0202' },
        BuildCostMass = 500,
        BuildCostEnergy = 2550,
        StorageMass = 0,
    },
    {
        -- T3 land factory. Same differential rule as above:
        --   T2 land 350m/1700e -> T3 land 850m/4200e => charged 500m/2500e
        -- Twice the T2 step, and the charge is atomic (FactoryQueue takes it in
        -- one go), so 2500e must stay under the army energy cap of the round it
        -- is meant to be bought in — see the energy-storage note below.
        -- Storage zeroed (stock 320).
        ids = { 'ueb0301', 'uab0301', 'urb0301', 'xsb0301' },
        BuildCostMass = 850,
        BuildCostEnergy = 4200,
        StorageMass = 0,
    },
    {
        -- T3 air factory.
        --   T2 air 500m/2550e -> T3 air 1150m/5800e => charged 650m/3250e
        ids = { 'ueb0302', 'uab0302', 'urb0302', 'xsb0302' },
        BuildCostMass = 1150,
        BuildCostEnergy = 5800,
        StorageMass = 0,
    },
    {
        -- T2 mobile missile launchers. Stock 180m/1350e is a 1:7.5 ratio; every
        -- other ground unit here is 1:5, and energy is the scarcer resource (the
        -- cap is a flat 3900 all game — nothing grants energy storage — against
        -- 100 e/s). 900 puts it on the same curve as the T2 heavy tanks, which
        -- are already 1:5 at stock and so need no override.
        ids = { 'uel0111', 'ual0111', 'url0111', 'xsl0111' },
        BuildCostEnergy = 900,
    },
    {
        -- T2 gunships. Stock is 1:20 on energy (3840-10000e), which against a
        -- 3900 cap makes the Seraphim one literally unqueueable and the rest a
        -- one-per-round purchase — the same energy trap the T1 bomber had. All
        -- four are brought to 1:5 on their own stock mass, so the factions keep
        -- their stock relative pricing (Stinger is the cheap light one, Vulthoo
        -- the 1800 HP heavy one) without energy being the real gate.
        ids = { 'uea0203' },              -- Stinger, 192 mass
        BuildCostEnergy = 960,
    },
    {
        ids = { 'uaa0203', 'ura0203' },   -- Specter / Renegade, 270 mass
        BuildCostEnergy = 1350,
    },
    {
        ids = { 'xsa0203' },              -- Vulthoo, 500 mass
        BuildCostEnergy = 2500,
    },
    {
        -- T2 fighter/bombers, the expansion-pack airframes (see the note in
        -- UnitTypes.lua about the dea/xaa/dra prefixes). Same 1:5 treatment.
        ids = { 'dea0202' },              -- Janus, 360 mass (stock 12600e)
        BuildCostEnergy = 1800,
    },
    {
        ids = { 'xaa0202' },              -- Swift Wind, 235 mass (stock 6750e)
        BuildCostEnergy = 1175,
    },
    {
        ids = { 'dra0202', 'xsa0202' },   -- Corsair / Notha, 420 mass (stock 8400e)
        BuildCostEnergy = 2100,
    },
    --------------------------------------------------------------------
    -- Tier 3 units. Same 1:5 mass:energy normalisation as tier 2, mass left at
    -- stock. NOTE the mass cap, which is the real gate up here: it is 216 at
    -- round 1 and grows +100/round, so a 480-mass Titan is unbuyable before
    -- round 4, an 840-mass Othuum before round 7, and the 1280-1750 mass
    -- "faction special"/"heavy air" picks before rounds 12-16. That is
    -- deliberate (they are meant to be a late-game statement) but it IS the dial
    -- to turn if T3 feels unreachable: either add BuildCostMass overrides here
    -- or raise the per-round storage step at the bottom of this file.
    --------------------------------------------------------------------
    {
        ids = { 'uel0303', 'url0303' },   -- Titan / Loyalist, 480 mass (stock 5250e)
        BuildCostEnergy = 2400,
    },
    {
        ids = { 'ual0303', 'xsl0303' },   -- Harbinger / Othuum, 840 mass (stock 9600e)
        BuildCostEnergy = 4200,
    },
    {
        -- T3 mobile heavy artillery, all four 800 mass at stock.
        ids = { 'uel0304', 'ual0304', 'url0304', 'xsl0304' },
        BuildCostEnergy = 4000,
    },
    {
        -- T3 mobile AA (the expansion-pack `d*lk*` ids — see UnitTypes.lua).
        ids = { 'delk002', 'dalk003', 'drlk001' },   -- 600 mass (stock 7500e)
        BuildCostEnergy = 3000,
    },
    {
        ids = { 'dslk004' },              -- Uyanah, 720 mass (stock 9600e)
        BuildCostEnergy = 3600,
    },
    -- No T3 mobile shield entry: there is no such row in the roster any more.
    -- `*l0309` — which used to be listed as one — are the T3 ENGINEERS, and being
    -- MOBILE CONSTRUCTION they showed up in the land AND air T3 build menus. See
    -- the note in lib/UnitTypes.lua before adding anything back here.
    {
        ids = { 'xel0305', 'xrl0305' },   -- Percival / The Brick, 1280 mass (stock 14000e)
        BuildCostEnergy = 6400,
    },
    {
        ids = { 'xal0305' },              -- Sprite Striker, 700 mass (stock 25000e)
        BuildCostEnergy = 3500,
    },
    {
        ids = { 'xsl0305' },              -- Usha-Ah, 780 mass (stock 27000e)
        BuildCostEnergy = 3900,
    },
    {
        -- T3 air-superiority fighters, 450 mass. Stock 51200e is 1:114 — by far
        -- the worst energy trap in the game against a cap that starts at 3900.
        ids = { 'uea0303', 'uaa0303', 'ura0303', 'xsa0303' },
        BuildCostEnergy = 2250,
    },
    {
        ids = { 'uea0305', 'xra0305' },   -- Broadsword / Wailer, 1500 mass (stock 65000e)
        BuildCostEnergy = 7500,
    },
    {
        ids = { 'xaa0305' },              -- Restorer, 1200 mass (stock 64000e)
        BuildCostEnergy = 6000,
    },
    {
        ids = { 'xsa0304' },              -- Sinntha, 1750 mass (stock 180000e)
        BuildCostEnergy = 8750,
    },
    {
        -- T3 Point Defense (the UEF Ravager, given to every faction — see the
        -- Categories merge below and the AcuStructures comment in UnitTypes.lua).
        -- Stock 2000m/17600e is 3.7x the T2 Point Defense; priced instead at
        -- exactly 2x it (540m/3780e), which is the same step stock uses between
        -- T2 and T3 AA. Built directly by the ACU, so unlike a queued unit this
        -- drains gradually and is not bounded by the storage cap.
        ids = { 'xeb2306' },
        BuildCostMass = 1080,
        BuildCostEnergy = 7560,
    },
    --------------------------------------------------------------------
    -- Land experimentals (lib/Experimentals.lua). Built DIRECTLY by the ACU once
    -- it has the Tech 3 Engineering Suite, then handed to the builder's wave army
    -- the moment they complete. Kamikaze's pricing rule: MASS a quarter of stock,
    -- ENERGY a fifth of stock — so these are the one group in this file that
    -- overrides mass as well, and the one group that does NOT land on the ~1:5
    -- ratio everything else uses (a quarter of mass against a fifth of energy is
    -- 1:12.5 on the Fatboy).
    --
    -- Being ACU-built they drain gradually over BuildTime and stall when income
    -- runs dry, so — unlike a queued wave unit — neither the mass nor the energy
    -- storage cap can make them unreachable. What DOES bound them is plain
    -- income: 7000 mass at the map's few mass/second is a multi-round commitment
    -- during which the ACU builds nothing else. That is the dial to turn if they
    -- feel unreachable in play; BuildTime is deliberately left at stock because
    -- the T3Engineering enhancement raises the ACU's build rate to 100, which
    -- with Config.AcuBuildRateMult (4) puts the Fatboy at roughly two minutes of
    -- build time rather than the twenty it would be pre-upgrade.
    --------------------------------------------------------------------
    {
        ids = { 'uel0401' },              -- Fatboy,     stock 28000m / 350000e
        BuildCostMass = 7000,
        BuildCostEnergy = 70000,
    },
    {
        ids = { 'ual0401' },              -- Colossus,   stock 27500m / 343750e
        BuildCostMass = 6875,
        BuildCostEnergy = 68750,
    },
    {
        ids = { 'url0402' },              -- Monkeylord, stock 20000m / 260000e
        BuildCostMass = 5000,
        BuildCostEnergy = 52000,
    },
    {
        ids = { 'xsl0401' },              -- Ythotha,    stock 26500m / 330000e
        BuildCostMass = 6625,
        BuildCostEnergy = 66000,
    },
    {
        -- Per-round step: the mass-storage building lib/CoreStorage.lua spawns
        -- (hidden, invulnerable) on each Core at the start of every round holds
        -- this much, so the cap grows by this per round. Stock 500.
        --
        -- THE COST MATTERS TOO, because the ACU can build these itself: all four
        -- carry BUILTBYTIER2COMMANDER (verified in units.nx2), and they sit in
        -- the script's allowed set so CoreStorage's own spawns survive
        -- AddRestriction — which hands them to the player as a side effect. So a
        -- player past AdvancedEngineering can buy cap on demand, against a design
        -- where storage and not income is what gates tier 3. Doubled from stock
        -- 200m/1500e on Kamikaze's call (2026-08-08) rather than blocked outright:
        -- buying capacity stays a legal play, it just has to be worth 400 mass
        -- against the +100 cap it grants. Turn the price up further here if it
        -- still reads as the obvious opening.
        ids = { 'ueb1106', 'uab1106', 'urb1106', 'xsb1106' },
        BuildCostMass = 400,
        BuildCostEnergy = 3000,
        StorageMass = 100,
    },
    {
        -- Per-round ENERGY step, added 2026-07-26 alongside tier 3. Until now
        -- the army energy cap was a flat 3900 (the ACU's) for the whole game,
        -- which was already the stated blocker for T3 in README's open balance
        -- questions: FactoryQueue charges a queued unit's full energy price
        -- atomically, so nothing costing over 3900e could ever be queued however
        -- long you saved, and at 1:5 that capped a unit at 780 mass. lib/
        -- CoreStorage.lua now spawns one of these (hidden on the Core, exactly
        -- like the mass one) each round too, so the energy cap is
        -- 3900 + 500 x (round - 1): 5900 at round 5, 8400 at round 10, which is
        -- what the tier-3 prices above are pitched against. Stock is 10000.
        --
        -- Cost doubled from stock 250m/1200e for the same reason as the mass
        -- storage above — the ACU can build these too, and +500 energy cap on
        -- demand is worth more here than the sticker price suggests.
        ids = { 'ueb1105', 'uab1105', 'urb1105', 'xsb1105' },
        BuildCostMass = 500,
        BuildCostEnergy = 2400,
        StorageEnergy = 500,
    },
}

for i, group in OVERRIDES do
    for j, id in group.ids do
        -- A fresh Economy table per blueprint: StoreBlueprint keeps what it is
        -- given, so sharing one table across merges would alias them together.
        local economy = {}
        if group.BuildCostMass ~= nil then economy.BuildCostMass = group.BuildCostMass end
        if group.BuildCostEnergy ~= nil then economy.BuildCostEnergy = group.BuildCostEnergy end
        if group.StorageMass ~= nil then economy.StorageMass = group.StorageMass end
        if group.StorageEnergy ~= nil then economy.StorageEnergy = group.StorageEnergy end
        UnitBlueprint {
            Merge = true,
            BlueprintId = id,
            Economy = economy,
        }
    end
end

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The one NON-Economy merge in this file: give every faction the UEF Ravager.
--
-- The stock game has exactly one T3 Point Defense, `xeb2306`, and an ACU's
-- Economy.BuildableCategory is faction-scoped ("BUILTBYTIER3COMMANDER CYBRAN",
-- UEL0001_unit.bp:227-231), so only a UEF ACU can reach it. Appending the other
-- three faction categories makes every ACU's expression match. The Ravager keeps
-- its UEF model, name and icon for everyone — cosmetic, and intended.
--
-- WHY THE EXPLICIT NUMERIC INDICES: BlueprintMerged (lua/system/Blueprints.lua:91)
-- merges KEY BY KEY, so a plain `Categories = { 'AEON', ... }` would overwrite
-- entries 1..3 of the stock 17-entry list — destroying "BUILTBYTIER3COMMANDER",
-- "BUILTBYTIER3ENGINEER" and "CQUEMOV". Writing indices 18..20 appends with no
-- gap. If a FAF patch ever changes the stock Categories list length this silently
-- overwrites or leaves a hole: re-check the count in units.nx2 before trusting it.
UnitBlueprint {
    Merge = true,
    BlueprintId = 'xeb2306',
    Categories = {
        [18] = 'AEON',
        [19] = 'CYBRAN',
        [20] = 'SERAPHIM',
    },
}

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Take the Core's own blueprint out of the UEF ACU's build menu.
--
-- THE BUG (game 27565454, reported 2026-08-07): a UEF player finished the
-- T3Engineering enhancement and could then build a second T3 power generator —
-- the Core. Config.CoreBlueprint IS ueb1301, and LineWars-2p_script.lua adds
-- categories.ueb1301 to the `allowed` set so the map's own AddRestriction does
-- not destroy the Core the script spawns. That exemption is necessary, but it
-- also stops being a restriction: stock ueb1301 carries BUILTBYTIER3COMMANDER
-- and UEF, and every UEF ACU's Economy.BuildableCategory contains
-- "BUILTBYTIER3COMMANDER UEF", so the moment the tier-3 gate lifts the Core
-- appears in the menu. It pays a stock 2500 e/s against a Core deliberately
-- throttled to CoreEnergyBase (500), so one build is five Cores' worth of
-- income. It showed up alongside the ACU experimentals for one shared reason:
-- T3Engineering unlocks both in the same menu at the same instant.
--
-- The fix is at the other end — a blueprint that no ACU can express is one the
-- restriction system never has to catch, so the Core stays script-spawnable
-- while being unbuildable by anyone. A merge cannot DELETE a field, so the two
-- offending entries are overwritten in place with an inert marker rather than
-- removed; a Categories list may hold a string that means nothing to the engine
-- (this one now yields categories.LINEWARSCORE, unused but a handy handle).
-- BUILTBYTIER3ENGINEER goes too: engineers are restricted today, so it costs
-- nothing to stop depending on that staying true.
--
-- Same numeric-index caveat as the Ravager above, and worse here because these
-- indices OVERWRITE rather than append: stock ueb1301 is a 15-entry list with
-- BUILTBYTIER3COMMANDER at 1 and BUILTBYTIER3ENGINEER at 2 (checked in
-- units.nx2, 2026-08-08). If a FAF patch reorders that list this silently
-- deletes the wrong two categories — STRUCTURE or UEF going missing would be
-- the loud version. Re-check before trusting it after a patch.
UnitBlueprint {
    Merge = true,
    BlueprintId = 'ueb1301',
    Categories = {
        [1] = 'LINEWARSCORE',
        [2] = 'LINEWARSCORE',
    },
}

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Take reclaim off the Aeon Harbinger at load time.
--
-- Kamikaze saw one picking over mass wrecks instead of fighting. It is the only
-- blueprint in the entire roster that can: of the 105 ids in UnitTypes, ual0303
-- alone has RULEUCC_Reclaim = true and a BuildRate (5), checked against
-- units.nx2 on 2026-08-08.
--
-- This is the SECOND of two mechanisms, and the runtime one is the primary:
-- WaveSpawner.SuppressReclaim calls RemoveCommandCap on every unit entering a
-- wave army, which is mod-proof where this merge is not (a map .bp loses to
-- mods — see the header). This one is here because what was actually issuing
-- the reclaim order was never identified, so a load-time block that never
-- depends on our code reaching the unit is worth having as well. Belt and
-- braces, the same pattern the experimentals tier gate uses.
--
-- BlueprintMerged (lua/system/Blueprints.lua:91) recurses into nested tables
-- and copies `false` like any other value — only the '__nil' sentinel deletes —
-- so this changes that single flag and leaves the rest of CommandCaps stock,
-- exactly as the Enhancements merge below does for the ACU.
--
-- NOT DONE BY ZEROING Economy.BuildRate, which looks like the more direct fix
-- and is a trap: it would leave the ORDER acceptable and merely make it take
-- forever, so a Harbinger told to reclaim would stand over a wreck doing
-- nothing for the rest of the game instead of being briefly distracted. Remove
-- the ability, not the speed.
UnitBlueprint {
    Merge = true,
    BlueprintId = 'ual0303',
    General = {
        CommandCaps = {
            RULEUCC_Reclaim = false,
        },
    },
}

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ACU tech-3 upgrade (the T3Engineering enhancement): 2400 mass -> 1500.
--
-- Not an Economy merge, so it cannot ride in OVERRIDES above (that loop only
-- forwards the four Economy fields, and tools/gen-units-md.py parses the table's
-- literal shape). Enhancement costs are absolute — the differential rule that
-- governs factory upgrades does not apply here, so 1500 is what the player pays.
--
-- BlueprintMerged recurses into nested tables (lua/system/Blueprints.lua:101),
-- so naming only BuildCostMass leaves the rest of the enhancement — Prerequisite
-- (AdvancedEngineering, itself 800m/21000e), BuildTime, NewBuildRate, Icon,
-- BuildableCategoryAdds — at stock. Same reason the string-keyed table is safe
-- where the Ravager's array-like Categories was not.
--
-- Energy is deliberately left at stock 50000: Kamikaze asked for the mass price
-- only. Unlike a factory upgrade, an enhancement is paid off over its BuildTime
-- rather than charged atomically, so it is not gated by the army energy cap —
-- but at 100 e/s of base income it is still the long pole in the upgrade.
for i, acu in { 'uel0001', 'ual0001', 'url0001', 'xsl0001' } do
    UnitBlueprint {
        Merge = true,
        BlueprintId = acu,
        Enhancements = {
            T3Engineering = {
                BuildCostMass = 1500,
            },
        },
    }
end

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Give the ACUs a brake. Fixes the movement stutter seen in the 2026-08-08
-- playtest: the ACU slows sharply as it nears its destination, then creeps the
-- last stretch in a series of forward jerks.
--
-- THE CAUSE. lib/AcuRules.lua runs the ACU at Config.AcuMoveSpeedMult (4x), so
-- top speed is 1.7 -> 6.8. Braking does not come along: SetSpeedMult, SetAccMult
-- and SetTurnMult are the ONLY motion multipliers the engine exposes (checked
-- against every Set*Mult in lua.nx2, 2026-08-08), and none of them is a brake.
-- Stopping distance scales as v^2/2b, so 4x speed wants ~16x the room.
--
-- And the room is not there. In the stock Physics block (UEL0001_unit.bp,
-- identical in the other three) MaxBrake is ABSENT — not low, missing. The
-- near-identical SACU (UEL0301) declares MaxBrake = 2.2 alongside the same
-- MaxAcceleration; the four ACUs declare none at all, because stock they never
-- move fast enough to care. Whether the engine then falls back to
-- MaxAcceleration or to something smaller was not established, and does not
-- change the fix: either way it is a stock-speed brake on a 4x unit, and the
-- arrival behaviour degrades. (The exact shape of that degradation — arriving
-- short and creeping, versus overshooting and, with MaxSpeedReverse = 0,
-- arcing back around on TurnRadius = 10 — is discussed in lib/AcuRules.lua.)
--
-- WHY 10, AND HOW TO RETUNE IT. The number that matters for feel is time to
-- stop from top speed, v/b, not stopping distance — and stock FA land units all
-- sit in a 0.5-1.0 second band. At the boosted speed of 6.8:
--
--     MaxBrake   time to stop   coast distance
--        30          0.23 s         0.77      first try; too snappy
--        20          0.34 s         1.16
--        15          0.45 s         1.54
--     >> 10          0.68 s         2.31   << here: mid-band
--         8          0.85 s         2.89
--       6.8          1.00 s         3.40      slowest still in family
--      ~1.7          4.00 s        13.60      the original bug
--
--   for reference: Striker UEL0201 3.4/3.4 = 1.00 s, Percival UEL0303
--   3.8/3.8 = 1.00 s, Engineer UEL0101 4.5/9 = 0.50 s (brake > accel is the
--   normal idiom — do NOT just mirror MaxAcceleration the way the SACU does).
--
-- 30 was the first value shipped. Game 27570392 confirmed it killed the stutter
-- outright, but it halted the ACU abruptly — 0.23 s is 2-4x snappier than
-- anything else in the game. 10 walks that back into the band. Going much below
-- 6.8 starts spending the margin that fixed the bug.
--
-- Note the ladder above assumes the engine does NOT scale MaxBrake by
-- SetAccMult. If it does, every time above is 4x shorter and the whole ladder
-- shifts; that was never established, because at 30 both readings are "abrupt"
-- and neither can be told apart by eye. If 10 still feels abrupt, that is the
-- answer, and the band to try is 2-4 rather than 6.8-15.
--
-- LOAD-TIME ONLY, AND MOD-FRAGILE. There is no runtime brake setter, so this
-- cannot be tuned by the "/acu" chat command the way the three multipliers can
-- — changing it costs a relaunch. And per the header at the top of this file, a
-- map .bp loses to mods: with a unit-overhaul mod active this merge is
-- overridden away and the stutter comes back, in which case the lever left is
-- lowering Config.AcuMoveSpeedMult.
--
-- If MaxBrake alone does not settle it, the runtime lever comes first: "/acu 4
-- 2 4" drops acceleration below speed so each correction is gentler. Only if
-- the ACU is seen visibly OVERSHOOTING and arcing back is TurnRadius = 10 the
-- suspect — left at stock here so one variable moves at a time. It is far
-- larger than the ACU's kinematic turn radius (~1.1 at stock speed), so it is
-- an approach/steering distance rather than a geometric minimum, and 10 map
-- units is 1.5 seconds of travel at 6.8 speed. Tanks ship 0.
for i, acu in { 'uel0001', 'ual0001', 'url0001', 'xsl0001' } do
    UnitBlueprint {
        Merge = true,
        BlueprintId = acu,
        Physics = {
            MaxBrake = 10,
        },
    }
end
